  "prompt": "Which of these calls does NOT mutate the original array?\n\n**A.** const copy = items.map((x) => x * 2)\n**B.** items.splice(0, 1)\n**C.** items.sort()\n**D.** items.reverse()",
  "instructions": [
    "Choose the single best answer."
  ],
  "constraints": [],
  "options": [
    "const copy = items.map((x) => x * 2)",
    "items.splice(0, 1)",
    "items.sort()",
    "items.reverse()"
  ],
  "correctOptionIndex": 0,
  "examples": [],
  "hints": [
    "Think about which method’s job is to BUILD a new array.",
    "ES2023 introduced spelled-out \"to\" variants for a reason."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "summary": "The correct answer is A — const copy = items.map((x) => x * 2)",
    "answer": "const copy = items.map((x) => x * 2)",
    "reasoning": [
      "The mutators are: push, pop, shift, unshift, splice, sort, reverse, fill, copyWithin.",
      "The accessors/iterators (map, filter, slice, concat, flat) return new arrays.",
      "ES2023 added non-mutating twins for the dangerous ones: toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced, with."
    ],
    "whyNot": [
      "items.splice(0, 1) — splice removes elements in place — the classic mutator.",
      "items.sort() — sort sorts in place and returns the same array reference.",
      "items.reverse() — reverse reverses in place (use toReversed for a copy on modern runtimes)."
    ]
  },
